  {
    "iteration": 8,
    "phase": "claude",
    "action": "finalize",
    "rationale": "Selected: three doublets at 3,500 m (117 C reservoir, ~114 C wellhead) with direct plate-HX heating at 100 C, a 10 MW single-effect absorption chiller driven by the brine, and a 2.5 MW high-temperature heat pump for cold-spell top-up. Reasoning: (1) the 330-day rule makes one doublet infeasible and the 40 C brine-cooling cap limits each doublet to ~13.4 MW, so the 30 MW peak needs three doublets regardless of depth; (2) at the specified 2,500 m the brine is 85 C, below the 100 C supply, forcing a 31 MW heat pump and 36 GWh/yr of electricity (COP 3.8, 86 EUR/MWh); (3) 3,000 m is still just below the 103 C needed for direct supply and is the worst of both worlds (92 EUR/MWh); (4) 3,500 m enables direct supply and full-COP absorption cooling, cutting electricity to 7.8 GWh/yr (COP 17.3) at 63.8 EUR/MWh; (5) 4,000 m adds well cost without raising the cap-limited heat per doublet (70.7 EUR/MWh); (6) two doublets at 3,500 m fail on 16 peak days and during maintenance; (7) absorption cooling saves 4.3 GWh/yr and 0.65 MEUR/yr versus electric chillers. ESP set at 1,000 m keeps the intake 40 bar above the CH4 bubble point; tubing velocity 2.3 m/s is below the API erosional limit; CO2 corrosion (8 mm/yr un-mitigated) is handled with inhibitor and GRE-lined tubing. Trade-off: higher well capex (3 x 3,780 m MD) against the lowest electricity use and highest geothermal share of all feasible options.",
